ke the late Mugals, restricted to Muslims. Unbelievers were to be kept subdued, and be made to pay the traditional poll tax. In legitimizing their conquest, the Durrani seem to have followed the Sunni school of law of ma\\u1e0f\\u0101hib. The presence of Shiite practioners in Khorasan seem to have been tolerated. Pitshtunwali, a legal and moral code that determines social order and responsibilities in Pashtun culture was at odds with the formalized Islamic code, having existed before the islamic conquest of the 7th century and enduring to the present day in the Pashtun border regions."}]', 'others': '{"usage.total_tokens": 914, "usage.prompt_tokens": 787}'}
